## 1 — parse_int in std/str.oo — OPEN (uncapped fold)

Landed this wave. Prior audits (`sprint_int_open_prove.oot`, `sprint_residuals_rollup.oot`) said it was absent. HYG `sprint_hyg_close.oot`: `str.oo` 100 → 131 (+31, parse_int). `std/core/str.oo` still has no `parse_int`.

Proof In (`ooda/std/str.oo`)

- L11 beat: "folds a leading `-` and ASCII digits 0-9 into Int (`v*10+n`)."
- L15 residual: "decimal ASCII only; no overflow check."
- L103–130: `pub fn parse_int`. Comment: "Digit fold only; do not call parse_int (recursion)."
- L105 `n = chars_len(s)` — no `if n > K`.
- L115–128 `while i < n` — every char, digit or not.
- Digit arm is `v = v * 10 + d`. No `if digits > 19`. No `if v > (MAX-d)/10`.
- Non-digits are skipped (not Err). Empty and `"-"` return 0.
- `i` always `i + 1`. Not an infinite loop. Unbounded in **input length**, not iterations-without-progress.

Twin (compiler, not std): `ooda/oodac/tc_parse_int.oo:12-38` is the same fold. Tokenize SKIP/TOK `i = parse_int(p[1])` uses that twin (`sprint_mem_caps.oot`). Scripts that import `std/str.oo` get this one. `harness_f26_residual_smoke.oo:101-109` still uses a local `parse_digits` (same `v*10+d`, no cap).

Heap (honest)

- The accumulator is one Int. That is **not** a growing String.
- Each `char_at` is `oo_char_at` (`chs_rt_str.c:198-214`): `utf8_byte_index` + `oo_str_alloc_payload` of 1 ASCII byte. Live C does **not** intern (check_cost_fast.oot intern claim is not this `oo_char_at`).
- Sequential `char_at` hits the utf8 cache (`chs_rt_str.c:176-186`) so time is Θ(n), not Θ(n²) on the index walk. `chars_len` is a prior Θ(n) walk.
- If the compiler emits `oo_str_release` on each 1-char temp, extra RSS is O(1). If it does not, n header mallocs (`sizeof(OoStrHeader)+len+1`). A 1 MiB digit string leaked that way is tens of MB, not 12 GiB.
- `repeat` in the same file is capped (`n<=1024`, L64-66). `parse_int` has no sister cap.

Wrap

- Int is C `long long` (i64). 20+ digits wrap. Garbage text can stay 0 (non-digits skipped) or wrap to a small value.
- That is a correctness / FAIL-never-fires hole on RSS parsers. It is **not** unbounded heap.

Not 12 GiB. Check `main.oo` does not import `std/str.oo`. Closing this cap would not move the B8 pin.

## 2 — ci_product rail loop — not a huge String

`.sh` is the product rail. `.oo` is a short twin with **no** rail loop. `ci_product_rails.oo` is absent (`sprint_hyg_close.oot`).

`.sh` loop (`ooda/scripts/ci_product.sh:73-222`)

- `for rail in \` … `pure_build_fp_smoke.sh` — **132** names (L74–205). Word list is a few KB.
- Body: `"$ROOT/scripts/$rail" >"$TMPDIR/ci_$rail.out" 2>"$TMPDIR/ci_$rail.err"` (L216).
- That is **file redirect**, not `out = out + stdout`.
- `pass` / `bad` print one line. `fail` is an Int counter.
- Post-loop: `fixed_point.sh` also redirected (L224). Then `grep -rq` over `$TMPDIR/ci_*.err` and `ci_*.out` (L237). Grep streams files.
- Logs are **not** unlinked. Disk can grow; parent bash RSS does not hold 132 full logs.
- `p7_check_main_smoke.sh` and `harness_f26_residual_smoke.sh` are **not** in the 132. This loop does not re-run THE 12 GiB check by name.
- `bootstrap_no_cargo.sh` (L62) still rebuilds `oodac/oodac`. Long rail / race residual. Not a String concat.

`.oo` (`ooda/scripts/ci_product.oo`, 47 lines)

- Beats claim "every listed rail exits 0". Body runs purity + shadow + `bootstrap_no_cargo` + `fixed_point` only. **No** `for` / **no** 132-name list.
- `check()` (`std/scenario.oo:29-38`) is `process_exec` then drop. `oo_sys_exec` (`chs_rt_sys.c:289-292`) returns `oo_str_lit("")` on success — **does not capture child stdout**. Redirects on L41/L43 are belt-and-suspenders, not a heap sink.
- Command strings are path-sized concat. Not a growing log.

If a later `.oo` rails file did `log = log + process_exec(...)` or glued 132 outputs into one String, that would be a bomb. That file is not on disk.

ci_product was **not** re-run this turn (INT: long rail / race). This section is source-only.

## 3 — tc_call_let still builds full ret_table — OPEN

B6 residual in SPRINT is still the live shape.

Proof still paid (`ooda/oodac/tc_call_let.oo`)

- L12 residual comment: "ret_table still built; cap names skip the per-call scan."
- L20 **every entry**: `let ret_table: String = build_fn_ret_table(toks);`
- L21–41 `aliases = aliases + aname + ":" + … + "\t"` — second unbounded String.
- L71–83 annotated `let x: Ann = f(`: `cap_builtin_ret` first; else `call_ret_type_at(..., ret_table, aliases)`.
- L106–115 unannotated `let x = f(`: `cap_builtin_ret` only. `gty` is **dropped**. No `call_ret_type_at`. No fail-closed. Table at L20 was already built.
- `check_drive.oo:111` still calls `typecheck_let_ann_call_init(toks)` on every `run_check_core_binds` (np>12 = once per module body).

`build_fn_ret_table` (`ooda/oodac/tc_tables_ret.oo:12-53`)

- `let mut table = "\t";` then `table = table + fname + ":" + ret + "\t"` per `fn` with ARROW IDENT.
- That is **whole-table String concat**, O(F²) copies for F named returns. Not `List[String]`.
- `resolve_type_alias` (`tc_types_core.oo:12-49`) then `contains` + char walk of that String.

cap_builtin_ret (`tc_call_ret.oo:218-248`) does not skip L20. It only skips the per-call `ret_table` scan **after** the table exists.

Same full build on the check rail (independent copies, not shared):

| Caller | Site | When |
|--------|------|------|
| `typecheck_let_ann_call_init` | `tc_call_let.oo:20` | every module |
| `typecheck_return_and_assign_calls` | `tc_call_ret.oo:14` | every module |
| `ann_prep` → `typecheck_ann_and_return_lits` | `tc_ann_prep.oo:16` | every module |
| `typecheck_call_*` siblings | `tc_call_{eq,order,binop,logic,arg_prep}.oo:14-15` | every module |
| `typecheck_field_method` | `tc_struct_method.oo:15` | every module |
| unary | `tc_ops_unary.oo:15` and `:92` | twice per module |
| `if g()` / `while g()` | `tc_control_cond.oo:200` | **inside** the cond walk (`n>1000` skips the whole fn) |
| untyped `let x = g()` | `tc_tables.oo:208` | **inside** the let walk |

np>12 (`check_mod.oo:53-84`) tokenizes **per module**, so each table is module-sized, not a 150-file concat. Still: one module pays this builder ~12 times, plus in-loop rebuilds on cond/let. That is check **churn**. It is not proved to be the 12 GiB (B8 leftover is still the named pin). Killing L20 alone would not be a new RSS without a new measure.

